1
00:00:01,805 --> 00:00:03,763
"Siblings"

2
00:00:06,182 --> 00:00:08,265
Come on, move!
Move!

3
00:00:08,300 --> 00:00:11,020
<i>My worst fears were about</i>
<i>to be realized.</i>

4
00:00:11,032 --> 00:00:13,818
<i>There was one event I could</i>
<i>not afford to be late for,</i>

5
00:00:13,853 --> 00:00:14,928
<i>and this was it.</i>

6
00:00:16,035 --> 00:00:17,851
Congratulations.

7
00:00:17,891 --> 00:00:20,748
We just elected you chairwoman
of the winter wreath sale.

8
00:00:22,820 --> 00:00:25,352
<i>Yep, when you showed up late</i>
<i>for Booster Club,</i>

9
00:00:25,433 --> 00:00:27,302
<i>you paid the price.</i>

10
00:00:27,666 --> 00:00:30,435
The best part is you get to keep
all the wreaths at your place,

11
00:00:30,475 --> 00:00:32,660
so your whole house
will smell amazing.

12
00:00:32,881 --> 00:00:34,006
And it'll last for months,

13
00:00:34,066 --> 00:00:35,923
because you never find
all the needles.

14
00:00:38,230 --> 00:00:39,075
That's the sap.

15
00:00:39,420 --> 00:00:41,725
You'll want to have a lot of
turpentine around the house.

16
00:00:41,856 --> 00:00:44,827
If you cut it with water,
it won't make your palms so raw.

17
00:00:45,192 --> 00:00:48,224
Hey, my girls.
Did you finish your wreaths?

18
00:00:48,329 --> 00:00:50,359
Shelly showed me
how to use a glue gun.

19
00:00:50,394 --> 00:00:52,286
Look what
a great a job she did.

20
00:00:52,321 --> 00:00:53,300
Ooh.

21
00:00:54,003 --> 00:00:55,117
Show your brother.

22
00:00:55,603 --> 00:00:57,102
- Sean!
- Sean!

23
00:00:57,204 --> 00:01:00,497
- Hey, hey, you two!
- Can I have a ride?

24
00:01:00,775 --> 00:01:03,768
No problem.
You too, milkshake.

25
00:01:06,715 --> 00:01:09,106
<i>After all the crap that had</i>
<i>been thrown at me that night,</i>

26
00:01:09,141 --> 00:01:10,641
<i>the sight of</i>
<i>those three kids</i>

27
00:01:10,721 --> 00:01:12,752
<i>lovingly frolicking</i>
<i>around the room</i>

28
00:01:13,574 --> 00:01:15,082
<i>really bugged the hell</i>
<i>out of me.</i>

29
00:01:15,547 --> 00:01:18,123
<i>I didn't really put my finger</i>
<i>on why till I got home.</i>

30
00:01:20,085 --> 00:01:21,321
<i>And then it hit me--</i>

31
00:01:21,611 --> 00:01:24,170
<i>I've never seen my kids</i>
<i>frolic together.</i>

32
00:01:24,230 --> 00:01:26,708
<i>It's like they were three</i>
<i>strangers in a bus station</i>

33
00:01:26,747 --> 00:01:28,179
<i>just waiting</i>
<i>to get out of here.</i>

34
00:01:31,792 --> 00:01:33,084
<i>Maybe they didn't</i>
<i>hang out together</i>

35
00:01:33,119 --> 00:01:35,076
<i>'cause Sue and Axl</i>
<i>had nothing in common.</i>

36
00:01:35,210 --> 00:01:36,594
So I'm trying out
for wrestlettes,

37
00:01:36,646 --> 00:01:39,883
which is wrestling cheerleaders,
which is actually easier

38
00:01:39,932 --> 00:01:41,431
to get into
than regular cheerleaders

39
00:01:41,466 --> 00:01:43,883
because it's all hot in the gym
where wrestling is,

40
00:01:43,963 --> 00:01:45,950
and there's not really
a crowd to cheer to.

41
00:01:46,021 --> 00:01:47,947
<i>Or maybe it's 'cause</i>
<i>Brick's so much younger.</i>

42
00:01:48,490 --> 00:01:51,232
<i>Or maybe 'cause Brick's</i>
<i>so much... Brick.</i>

43
00:01:51,267 --> 00:01:53,080
Brick,
what are you doing?

44
00:01:53,094 --> 00:01:54,870
It usually copies me,

45
00:01:55,282 --> 00:01:58,922
but sometimes if I look
<i>very</i> carefully...

46
00:02:01,123 --> 00:02:02,234
it doesn't.

47
00:02:02,804 --> 00:02:04,101
Mom!

48
00:02:04,572 --> 00:02:06,553
<i>And when the three of them are</i>
<i>forced to do anything together,</i>

49
00:02:06,908 --> 00:02:08,681
<i>that's when the fun</i>
<i>really starts.</i>

50
00:02:08,682 --> 00:02:10,427
Feels like we've been
driving forever.

51
00:02:10,462 --> 00:02:12,492
Feels like we've been
driving forever.

52
00:02:12,527 --> 00:02:14,226
- Cut it out, Axl.
- Cut it out, Axl.

53
00:02:14,261 --> 00:02:15,370
- That's not funny.
- That's not funny.

54
00:02:15,371 --> 00:02:17,172
- You know, I hate it when you do that!
- You know, I hate it when you do that!

55
00:02:17,207 --> 00:02:19,208
- Why are you so mean?
- Why are you so mean?

56
00:02:19,377 --> 00:02:20,549
Why are you so mean?

57
00:02:20,584 --> 00:02:22,319
- I'm just ignoring you.
- I'm just ignoring you.

58
00:02:22,650 --> 00:02:23,440
I'm just ignoring you.

59
00:02:23,491 --> 00:02:24,873
- Wait, what are you doing?
- Wait, what are you doing?

60
00:02:24,908 --> 00:02:26,518
- Brick, knock it off. That's lame.
- Brick, knock it off. That's lame.

61
00:02:26,578 --> 00:02:28,250
- It doesn't work that way. Quit it!
- It doesn't work that way. Quit it!

62
00:02:28,295 --> 00:02:30,198
- Ugh!
- Ugh!

63
00:02:31,694 --> 00:02:34,024
What is wrong
with you people?

64
00:02:34,059 --> 00:02:37,561
Why are you watching the same
show on three different TVs?

65
00:02:38,374 --> 00:02:40,133
We can't agree
on the volume.

66
00:02:42,457 --> 00:02:45,134
<i>The next morning, Mike met</i>
<i>Aunt Edie down at the quarry.</i>

67
00:02:45,169 --> 00:02:46,851
It's really brutal
this year, Edie.

68
00:02:47,148 --> 00:02:49,841
Most of the guys will get
almost no bonus at all.

69
00:02:50,118 --> 00:02:52,914
<i>Ever since she retired</i>
<i>as a bookkeeper 14 years ago,</i>

70
00:02:52,949 --> 00:02:55,126
<i>Aunt Edie came to</i>
<i>Mike's office every December</i>

71
00:02:55,161 --> 00:02:56,343
<i>to help</i>
<i>close out the books.</i>

72
00:02:56,958 --> 00:02:58,671
I'm hoping we can
massage the numbers

73
00:02:58,706 --> 00:03:00,318
to avoid triggering layoffs,

74
00:03:00,319 --> 00:03:01,884
but it's gonna be tight.

75
00:03:08,150 --> 00:03:09,265
What are you doing
with that?

76
00:03:09,604 --> 00:03:11,346
I gotta plug in Ginny.

77
00:03:15,232 --> 00:03:17,109
You brought Ginny
to the quarry?

78
00:03:21,316 --> 00:03:23,575
Well, the wheelchair
won't fit into the trailer,

79
00:03:23,646 --> 00:03:25,162
and I'm afraid
to leave her home alone

80
00:03:25,224 --> 00:03:28,394
because I think
Ginny is starting to lose it.

81
00:03:28,395 --> 00:03:29,950
Oh, no kidding.
Sorry to hear that.

82
00:03:30,356 --> 00:03:32,062
And then
there's something else.

83
00:03:32,260 --> 00:03:34,601
I think Ginny
is starting to lose it.

84
00:03:42,236 --> 00:03:43,647
Yeah, come in.

85
00:03:44,272 --> 00:03:45,650
Hey, madame chairwoman!

86
00:03:45,907 --> 00:03:48,272
Hey, Nancy.
What's up?

87
00:03:48,443 --> 00:03:50,535
I just came by because
people were complaining

88
00:03:50,570 --> 00:03:51,951
they hadn't
gotten their wreaths yet.

89
00:03:51,986 --> 00:03:53,808
I only brought 'em home
last night!

90
00:03:54,082 --> 00:03:56,419
You're doing awesome.

91
00:03:56,420 --> 00:03:58,035
I just set the bar
really high.

92
00:03:59,344 --> 00:04:02,373
Oh, those girls.

93
00:04:02,828 --> 00:04:05,501
I'm taking Dotty to the mall
to get her ears pierced.

94
00:04:05,660 --> 00:04:08,007
She wants to be
just like her sister.

95
00:04:08,872 --> 00:04:09,599
Wow.

96
00:04:10,602 --> 00:04:12,650
They're really close,
aren't they?

97
00:04:13,201 --> 00:04:14,776
Why do you suppose
that is?

98
00:04:15,003 --> 00:04:16,464
Early on,
Joe and I decided

99
00:04:16,499 --> 00:04:18,261
our kids getting along
was a priority,

100
00:04:19,005 --> 00:04:20,393
but it takes
a lot of work.

101
00:04:20,906 --> 00:04:22,747
Damn. I was hoping it
was something

102
00:04:22,782 --> 00:04:24,341
you just crushed
into their cereal.

103
00:04:24,461 --> 00:04:25,313
From the beginning,

104
00:04:25,348 --> 00:04:26,756
when the kids
would get into an argument,

105
00:04:26,837 --> 00:04:29,036
I would get in there
and give them the proper tools

106
00:04:29,096 --> 00:04:31,777
- to resolve things.
- Yeah. Yeah. Sure.

107
00:04:31,778 --> 00:04:33,333
I mean, I do that.

108
00:04:33,988 --> 00:04:35,846
- Give it to me! Stop it!
- Aw. Unh-unh.

109
00:04:36,207 --> 00:04:37,209
Hey, knock it off!

110
00:04:37,244 --> 00:04:38,635
We're trying to watch TV.

111
00:04:40,695 --> 00:04:43,696
- And Mike supports you?
- Oh, yeah, sure.

112
00:04:44,032 --> 00:04:46,092
Hey, you heard your mom.
Knock it off!

113
00:04:48,139 --> 00:04:49,361
The important thing is

114
00:04:49,472 --> 00:04:51,728
we made sure that
they spent time together--

115
00:04:52,159 --> 00:04:55,662
Family dinners, game nights,
car trips, the obvious things.

116
00:04:55,810 --> 00:04:57,875
Oh, yeah. Um,
those things are obvious.

117
00:04:58,408 --> 00:05:02,103
<i>Dinners, game night, trips.</i>
<i>Dinners, game night...</i>

118
00:05:02,755 --> 00:05:04,322
<i>Damn it!</i>
<i>What was the last one?</i>

119
00:05:07,583 --> 00:05:08,944
Hi. How's it going?

120
00:05:10,119 --> 00:05:11,875
I had lunch
with Stan today.

121
00:05:11,963 --> 00:05:14,867
Corporate want another $10,000
out of the budget.

122
00:05:15,345 --> 00:05:17,401
Oh, and I think your Aunt Edie
is losing her mind.

123
00:05:17,503 --> 00:05:18,242
What?

124
00:05:18,324 --> 00:05:21,623
Throughout the day, she called me
"Mickey," "Milton," "Myrtle."

125
00:05:22,276 --> 00:05:24,479
Seriously, I think
she's going, you know, senile.

126
00:05:24,555 --> 00:05:26,155
No, she's not.

127
00:05:26,276 --> 00:05:28,935
All the women in our family get
a little loopy toward the end.

128
00:05:29,860 --> 00:05:32,728
Uh, what are you doing?
We having company or something?

129
00:05:32,729 --> 00:05:35,364
No. I don't know.
I just thought we'd eat in here.

130
00:05:35,833 --> 00:05:37,521
Did the kids
break the big TV?

131
00:05:37,751 --> 00:05:39,134
Hey, kids, get in here!

132
00:05:39,135 --> 00:05:41,868
No, no. Nobody broke the TV.
No, I just thought,

133
00:05:41,903 --> 00:05:44,498
you know, we don't have to eat
with the TV every night.

134
00:05:44,569 --> 00:05:46,364
We could just
sit and talk to each other.

135
00:05:46,531 --> 00:05:48,307
Whoa. Why are there plates
on the table?

136
00:05:48,405 --> 00:05:49,778
Because that's where
we're eating dinner.

137
00:05:49,962 --> 00:05:51,961
What? I don't
want to watch the little TV.

138
00:05:52,282 --> 00:05:53,890
Did someone break
the big TV?

139
00:05:53,925 --> 00:05:55,354
The TVs are fine.

140
00:05:55,389 --> 00:05:57,341
We're just not watching them
until after supper.

141
00:05:57,503 --> 00:06:00,293
Tonight, we're having a family
dinner in here for a change.

142
00:06:03,693 --> 00:06:04,866
Dad?

143
00:06:06,096 --> 00:06:09,140
You heard your mother.
Everybody... sit down.

144
00:06:10,866 --> 00:06:11,802
What is the big deal?

145
00:06:11,862 --> 00:06:13,337
We've eaten
at the table before.

146
00:06:16,608 --> 00:06:17,692
I don't have a chair.

147
00:06:17,807 --> 00:06:21,145
<i>Huh. I guess we hadn't</i>
<i>really eaten at the table</i>

148
00:06:21,180 --> 00:06:22,123
<i>since we had Brick.</i>

149
00:06:23,046 --> 00:06:25,110
Not a problem.
We have more chairs outside.

150
00:06:26,987 --> 00:06:28,441
Dad,
why are we doing this?

151
00:06:28,595 --> 00:06:31,313
Uh, maybe your mom and I
need to, uh, talk to you.

152
00:06:31,348 --> 00:06:33,378
- I don't know.
- Are you getting a divorce?

153
00:06:33,990 --> 00:06:35,580
Let's see how this goes.

154
00:06:38,962 --> 00:06:39,965
This'll be fun.

155
00:06:40,316 --> 00:06:41,531
You know,
this is actually

156
00:06:41,566 --> 00:06:43,730
how a lot of people
eat dinner every night.

157
00:06:43,765 --> 00:06:45,866
They sit
and they face each other

158
00:06:46,157 --> 00:06:47,122
and they ask
each other questions

159
00:06:47,192 --> 00:06:49,123
about how their day went.
Let's do that.

160
00:06:49,474 --> 00:06:52,541
So what was your high and
what was your low of the day?

161
00:06:52,752 --> 00:06:55,437
- My low is right now.
- Fine. I'll do it.

162
00:06:56,152 --> 00:06:59,107
My high is having dinner
here with my family.

163
00:06:59,142 --> 00:07:01,836
My low is the comment
that Axl just made.

164
00:07:02,300 --> 00:07:03,026
Sue.

165
00:07:04,220 --> 00:07:07,019
My high was that the guy who
sits next to me in science

166
00:07:07,054 --> 00:07:08,645
saw me
in the hallway today

167
00:07:08,680 --> 00:07:10,499
and seemed to sort of
recognize me.

168
00:07:10,890 --> 00:07:11,093
<i>Mm.</i>

169
00:07:11,655 --> 00:07:13,533
My low was that
I was wrong.

170
00:07:14,195 --> 00:07:14,882
He didn't.

171
00:07:15,031 --> 00:07:16,096
<i>Loser!</i>

172
00:07:17,100 --> 00:07:19,427
What did I say about
sneezing words at your sister?

173
00:07:19,697 --> 00:07:20,778
My low is realizing

174
00:07:20,813 --> 00:07:22,465
that my family never bought
a chair for me.

175
00:07:22,965 --> 00:07:25,221
My high is I can eat food
right off the table.

176
00:07:27,711 --> 00:07:30,039
Oh, wait.
I want to change my low.

177
00:07:30,074 --> 00:07:30,926
Okay.
All right, all right.

178
00:07:30,961 --> 00:07:32,586
Let's just forget
highs and lows,

179
00:07:32,621 --> 00:07:34,874
and we'll do
something different. Mm...

180
00:07:35,652 --> 00:07:38,237
Everybody, look at
the person on your left

181
00:07:38,589 --> 00:07:39,773
and say something nice.

182
00:07:42,193 --> 00:07:43,078
You mean well.

183
00:07:44,470 --> 00:07:45,361
Thank you.

184
00:07:46,957 --> 00:07:49,095
Axl? You do Sue.

185
00:07:54,408 --> 00:07:56,448
Your head is basically
the right size for your body.

186
00:07:57,207 --> 00:08:00,052
Really?
You're not just saying that?

187
00:08:00,346 --> 00:08:03,855
No, it's everything, like, below
the head that's got the problem.

188
00:08:04,278 --> 00:08:05,192
Shut up!

189
00:08:05,227 --> 00:08:06,712
You know what
shuts people up? TV.

190
00:08:06,713 --> 00:08:09,651
Listen, you kids
are not even trying!

191
00:08:10,002 --> 00:08:12,253
You're brother and sister,
and this is the only relationship

192
00:08:12,314 --> 00:08:14,397
that is going
to last your whole life.

193
00:08:14,678 --> 00:08:15,870
You know, someday,
your dad and I

194
00:08:15,928 --> 00:08:17,849
are not gonna be
around anymore,

195
00:08:18,060 --> 00:08:19,700
and you're only
gonna have each other.

196
00:08:19,735 --> 00:08:20,762
Are you dying?

197
00:08:21,958 --> 00:08:25,229
Oh, my--that's why we're eating
at the table. She's dying!

198
00:08:25,264 --> 00:08:27,430
No! I'm not dying.

199
00:08:27,431 --> 00:08:29,760
Oh, my God!
Dad's dying!

200
00:08:29,795 --> 00:08:31,573
Nobody is dying!

201
00:08:31,652 --> 00:08:33,569
Why can't we all just die now
and get it over with?!

202
00:08:33,604 --> 00:08:34,398
- Mike!
- Axl.

203
00:08:34,399 --> 00:08:34,932
What did I say?

204
00:08:37,891 --> 00:08:39,045
All right, that's it!

205
00:08:39,749 --> 00:08:40,569
That's it.

206
00:08:41,343 --> 00:08:44,209
You three are gonna start
loving each other right now

207
00:08:44,390 --> 00:08:46,522
because that's
what your mother wants!

208
00:08:50,360 --> 00:08:51,404
Right?
Is that what you want?

209
00:08:57,236 --> 00:08:59,575
<i>So despite</i>
<i>my best efforts,</i>

210
00:08:59,610 --> 00:09:01,650
<i>family dinner had wheezed</i>
<i>and choked itself</i>

211
00:09:01,685 --> 00:09:03,503
<i>to a final,</i>
<i>undignified death.</i>

212
00:09:04,144 --> 00:09:05,460
Can we go watch TV now?

213
00:09:07,745 --> 00:09:09,254
Yeah,
throw out your plates.

214
00:09:13,700 --> 00:09:14,931
Nice to have a change.

215
00:09:25,744 --> 00:09:26,875
So what's up with you?

216
00:09:27,412 --> 00:09:28,762
You going loopy
a little ahead of schedule?

217
00:09:29,081 --> 00:09:31,693
No, I just thought
if we ate dinner together,

218
00:09:31,728 --> 00:09:33,618
it might help the kids
to become friends.

219
00:09:34,019 --> 00:09:36,268
You know, I was talking
to Nancy Donahue today--

220
00:09:36,303 --> 00:09:37,757
Oh, man, here we go.

221
00:09:38,412 --> 00:09:40,545
Every time you talk to that woman,
there's always something.

222
00:09:40,546 --> 00:09:42,062
You run into her,
and the next thing I know,

223
00:09:42,153 --> 00:09:43,786
I can't eat white bread
anymore.

224
00:09:43,927 --> 00:09:45,833
Her kids are nuts
about each other.

225
00:09:45,868 --> 00:09:49,769
You see how they are together.
They're helpful, caring and...

226
00:09:50,034 --> 00:09:52,035
I've seen it.
It's unnatural--all that,

227
00:09:52,086 --> 00:09:53,544
"Can I help you?
You're so pretty."

228
00:09:54,039 --> 00:09:55,089
One of these days,
I swear to God,

229
00:09:55,124 --> 00:09:56,634
there's gonna be
a bloodbath in that house.

230
00:09:56,669 --> 00:09:58,580
I don't know.
I wonder if we've been lazy.

231
00:09:58,581 --> 00:10:00,009
How the hell
have we been lazy?

232
00:10:00,094 --> 00:10:02,307
In the last 15 years,
I think we've sat down twice.

233
00:10:02,308 --> 00:10:04,726
Face it, we have cut
corners as parents.

234
00:10:04,761 --> 00:10:06,552
We're tired.
We've been busy.

235
00:10:06,587 --> 00:10:08,657
We've slacked off
on a lot of the family stuff,

236
00:10:08,658 --> 00:10:10,376
and that's why
our kids aren't close.

237
00:10:10,560 --> 00:10:12,964
All right.
You're this worried about it?

238
00:10:12,999 --> 00:10:14,791
I can help you, but you know
what I'm gonna say.

239
00:10:14,826 --> 00:10:15,764
Oh, God.

240
00:10:15,896 --> 00:10:17,025
Sports!

241
00:10:17,467 --> 00:10:18,729
Okay.

242
00:10:19,703 --> 00:10:23,001
Okay, everybody.
This family is heading outside

243
00:10:23,262 --> 00:10:24,461
to play touch football.

244
00:10:24,732 --> 00:10:26,875
Wh--football? Why?

245
00:10:26,982 --> 00:10:28,667
I don't want
to play with her.

246
00:10:28,702 --> 00:10:30,170
But this is
my favorite commercial.

247
00:10:31,294 --> 00:10:33,178
What did Mom
do to you?

248
00:10:33,213 --> 00:10:34,908
What your mother did to me
is none of your business.

249
00:10:34,977 --> 00:10:36,410
The point is,
brothers and sisters

250
00:10:36,471 --> 00:10:37,881
are supposed
to appreciate each other.

251
00:10:37,882 --> 00:10:41,356
Your Aunt Edie wheels Aunt Ginny
into the quarry every day,

252
00:10:41,693 --> 00:10:42,793
and she doesn't
even know where she is.

253
00:10:43,376 --> 00:10:44,812
That's the kind
of love and support

254
00:10:44,871 --> 00:10:47,179
that I want to start seeing
around here. Let's move now.

255
00:10:53,293 --> 00:10:55,177
Yo, here's the pitch.
Here's the pitch.

256
00:10:55,776 --> 00:10:58,006
Here's the--no pitch. Okay.
It's a break. Never mind.

257
00:10:58,544 --> 00:10:59,818
Here you go.
Here you go. Here you go.

258
00:11:00,243 --> 00:11:00,903
Here you go.
Throw it!

259
00:11:03,418 --> 00:11:04,688
Okay,
a little indecisive there.

260
00:11:06,353 --> 00:11:06,824
Hike!

261
00:11:10,397 --> 00:11:13,064
<i>At first it was a little shaky.</i>
<i>But then...</i>

262
00:11:13,224 --> 00:11:14,664
I have an idea.

263
00:11:14,691 --> 00:11:16,108
Kids versus adults.

264
00:11:17,822 --> 00:11:20,087
All right, so Dad's peripheral
vision is starting to go,

265
00:11:20,088 --> 00:11:22,256
and Mom can't run too fast,
or she'll pee.

266
00:11:22,365 --> 00:11:24,805
So go deep and
to the left. Ready?

267
00:11:25,061 --> 00:11:25,681
Break!

268
00:11:31,117 --> 00:11:32,432
<i>After a few plays,</i>

269
00:11:32,522 --> 00:11:34,914
- <i>something weird happened.</i>
- Ooh! Ooh! Ooh! I did it!

270
00:11:34,949 --> 00:11:37,378
<i>Those kids were throwing</i>
<i>and chasing and laughing</i>

271
00:11:37,413 --> 00:11:38,780
<i>like a bunch of Donahues.</i>

272
00:11:38,781 --> 00:11:39,984
All right, Sue,
do 10 yards and out.

273
00:11:40,035 --> 00:11:41,215
Brick, 5-yard buttonhook.

274
00:11:41,285 --> 00:11:43,150
I'm gonna roll out right
and find you in the end zone.

275
00:11:43,865 --> 00:11:45,292
I've got a better idea.

276
00:11:45,655 --> 00:11:47,860
- Whoa, whoa!
- Car!

277
00:11:48,369 --> 00:11:49,194
Car, car, car?

278
00:11:50,600 --> 00:11:51,322
Oh, no, no, no, no!

279
00:11:51,357 --> 00:11:53,052
- Oh, no, no, no, no, no!
- Hey, whoa!

280
00:11:53,087 --> 00:11:54,997
- Oh, wait a minute!
- Oh, no!

281
00:11:54,998 --> 00:11:56,082
Look at you, you, you.

282
00:11:56,153 --> 00:11:57,946
- All right.
- Come on, come on!

283
00:11:58,176 --> 00:11:59,522
- They slick-dogged us.
- Come on!

284
00:11:59,569 --> 00:12:01,137
- Again!
- Yeah, no, no, no.

285
00:12:02,433 --> 00:12:04,233
Oh, boy. All right.

286
00:12:05,608 --> 00:12:07,824
<i>Later, the kids came inside</i>
<i>and had dessert together.</i>

287
00:12:07,911 --> 00:12:09,382
- Then jumped up...
- <i>And to my utter amazement,</i>

288
00:12:09,417 --> 00:12:11,842
<i>Axl didn't pretend</i>
<i>to commit suicide once.</i>

289
00:12:12,087 --> 00:12:14,864
<i>There were no two ways about it.</i>
<i>Mike was on to something.</i>

290
00:12:15,213 --> 00:12:17,446
<i>Suffice it to say,</i>
<i>the kids weren't the only ones</i>

291
00:12:17,526 --> 00:12:19,269
<i>who got along especially well</i>
<i>that night...</i>

292
00:12:19,728 --> 00:12:21,557
<i>which is good,</i>
<i>'cause the next day?</i>

293
00:12:21,830 --> 00:12:23,429
<i>Not so relaxing for Mike.</i>

294
00:12:28,466 --> 00:12:29,200
Hey, guys.

295
00:12:32,164 --> 00:12:32,737
Mike?

296
00:12:33,201 --> 00:12:36,428
We just got our bonus checks,
and we need to talk to you.

297
00:12:37,440 --> 00:12:40,002
Jim has prepared a statement
from all of us.

298
00:12:40,037 --> 00:12:42,222
Oh, man. Look, I know
it's not what you expected,

299
00:12:42,301 --> 00:12:43,841
but you've to understand
what we're going through.

300
00:12:43,876 --> 00:12:45,483
"In these difficult times,

301
00:12:45,935 --> 00:12:49,405
"it is rare to have a boss
who is not only generous

302
00:12:49,495 --> 00:12:51,882
but also is who a boss..."

303
00:12:52,825 --> 00:12:54,110
No, that can't be right.

304
00:12:54,511 --> 00:12:55,601
I told you to proofread it.

305
00:12:56,570 --> 00:12:59,760
Hold on. I was generous?
How generous was I?

306
00:12:59,929 --> 00:13:02,130
Oh, my God!

307
00:13:04,945 --> 00:13:06,616
Mike, we want you
to know

308
00:13:06,651 --> 00:13:08,496
you didn't just give us
money this year.

309
00:13:08,969 --> 00:13:10,828
You gave hope
back to our families.

310
00:13:13,210 --> 00:13:14,443
So what are you gonna do?

311
00:13:14,444 --> 00:13:17,292
I don't know. I guess I gotta
find a way to take back hope

312
00:13:17,361 --> 00:13:19,651
from a bunch of guys
who use dynamite for a living.

313
00:13:19,849 --> 00:13:20,403
Hey!

314
00:13:21,050 --> 00:13:23,664
- You guys ready for football?
- What's that now?

315
00:13:23,665 --> 00:13:25,875
Come on. Kids versus
the elderly. Let's go!

316
00:13:26,046 --> 00:13:29,432
Oh. Guys, we have both
had really hard days.

317
00:13:29,467 --> 00:13:30,807
Why don't you play
without us?

318
00:13:30,917 --> 00:13:32,940
But it won't be any fun
without you.

319
00:13:33,021 --> 00:13:35,283
<i>Yep,</i>
<i>the kids had such a good time</i>

320
00:13:35,365 --> 00:13:37,088
<i>they wanted to do it</i>
<i>the next night...</i>

321
00:13:39,074 --> 00:13:40,749
<i>and the next night...</i>

322
00:13:45,356 --> 00:13:46,761
<i>and the next night.</i>

323
00:13:47,241 --> 00:13:49,159
<i>Turns out there is</i>
<i>a way to bond your kids</i>

324
00:13:49,194 --> 00:13:50,684
<i>and have tons</i>
<i>of family fun together.</i>

325
00:13:50,978 --> 00:13:53,273
<i>The only trouble is,</i>
<i>it requires spending</i>

326
00:13:53,308 --> 00:13:54,355
<i>a whole lot of time</i>
<i>with them.</i>

327
00:13:54,463 --> 00:13:56,290
All right, let's let them
win quick

328
00:13:56,325 --> 00:13:58,334
and get back in here while
the couch is still warm.

329
00:14:08,004 --> 00:14:09,992
<i>Mike knew he was gonna</i>
<i>have to bite the bullet</i>

330
00:14:10,100 --> 00:14:11,516
<i>and fire Aunt Edie.</i>

331
00:14:15,964 --> 00:14:16,875
<i>Or was he?</i>

332
00:14:26,393 --> 00:14:31,069
I was just about ready to start
another round of checks.

333
00:14:31,199 --> 00:14:32,488
Listen, Aunt Edie...

334
00:14:34,136 --> 00:14:35,563
There's something
we gotta talk about.

335
00:14:36,003 --> 00:14:38,462
It's not easy. You've been
doing this annual closeout

336
00:14:38,497 --> 00:14:39,757
with me for a long time.

337
00:14:39,848 --> 00:14:41,747
I think the work
keeps me alive.

338
00:14:42,309 --> 00:14:43,229
That's nice.

339
00:14:43,372 --> 00:14:46,611
But you've earned
the right to retire.

340
00:14:46,936 --> 00:14:51,019
Thanks, doll! Should we get
started on those new checks?

341
00:14:51,118 --> 00:14:54,091
No, no checks!
Um, I'm trying to tell you

342
00:14:54,126 --> 00:14:55,676
that we won't be
needing you here anymore.

343
00:14:55,711 --> 00:14:56,747
Oh, I see.

344
00:14:57,224 --> 00:14:59,650
So you want to do the checks
this afternoon then?

345
00:15:01,206 --> 00:15:01,949
No.

346
00:15:02,362 --> 00:15:04,752
No, I don't need you
to write the checks ever.

347
00:15:05,199 --> 00:15:08,452
So from now on, I'll just
be balancing the books?

348
00:15:08,669 --> 00:15:11,534
No. No, no more balancing.
No more checks.

349
00:15:11,672 --> 00:15:14,711
Well, if I'm not writing checks
and not balancing the books,

350
00:15:14,751 --> 00:15:16,599
I won't be working
for you at all.

351
00:15:16,600 --> 00:15:18,799
That's right!
Exactly.

352
00:15:18,834 --> 00:15:21,404
It's time for you
to take Ginny and go home.

353
00:15:21,584 --> 00:15:23,887
Oh, I don't think
you mean that.

354
00:15:24,251 --> 00:15:24,903
Why don't I?

355
00:15:24,938 --> 00:15:27,889
Because if I go home now,
the work will pile up,

356
00:15:27,980 --> 00:15:29,646
and <i>we'll</i> get behind.

357
00:15:34,294 --> 00:15:37,094
Edie,
you're a great old gal.

358
00:15:37,095 --> 00:15:38,824
I think you're cute, too.

359
00:15:39,566 --> 00:15:43,664
Thanks.
But the job here is over.

360
00:15:44,620 --> 00:15:45,479
No more job.

361
00:15:46,570 --> 00:15:47,604
Job done.

362
00:15:48,375 --> 00:15:49,929
I'm letting you go.

363
00:15:51,255 --> 00:15:51,951
Do you get it?

364
00:15:52,067 --> 00:15:56,173
Oh, I get it. I know
what it means to be let go.

365
00:15:56,174 --> 00:15:56,807
You do?

366
00:15:57,428 --> 00:15:59,310
Now if we continue
on this,

367
00:15:59,345 --> 00:16:01,787
we are just never gonna
get these checks done.

368
00:16:04,291 --> 00:16:07,340
- Aunt Edie, you're fired.
- Fired?

369
00:16:07,375 --> 00:16:07,952
Yes.

370
00:16:08,176 --> 00:16:11,159
Oh!
This is very disappointing.

371
00:16:11,772 --> 00:16:14,234
What is? Tell me--tell me what
you think is disappointing.

372
00:16:14,269 --> 00:16:16,244
- That I'm fired!
- Oh!

373
00:16:17,118 --> 00:16:17,824
Thank God.

374
00:16:19,139 --> 00:16:22,110
Well,
should we get started now?

375
00:16:25,612 --> 00:16:28,836
You know, I think
I've actually got those checks

376
00:16:28,871 --> 00:16:30,008
pretty much
under control.

377
00:16:30,384 --> 00:16:34,338
But what I really do
need your help with...

378
00:16:35,573 --> 00:16:38,709
Management
is all over me

379
00:16:38,820 --> 00:16:41,396
about getting
this puzzle put together.

380
00:16:42,746 --> 00:16:44,011
Oh, my.

381
00:16:44,798 --> 00:16:46,145
This is a big one.

382
00:16:47,334 --> 00:16:48,115
Oh.

383
00:16:48,835 --> 00:16:51,676
Look at this.
I found a corner.

384
00:16:52,406 --> 00:16:53,029
Good girl.

385
00:17:00,314 --> 00:17:01,809
They're waiting for us.

386
00:17:08,822 --> 00:17:11,132
Ow. Damn.

387
00:17:11,133 --> 00:17:11,585
What?

388
00:17:11,620 --> 00:17:13,527
My knee went out
for a second.

389
00:17:14,027 --> 00:17:15,310
I'm gonna have to
sit this one out.

390
00:17:16,758 --> 00:17:20,148
Ohh. Well, Mom,
you're still coming, right?

391
00:17:20,461 --> 00:17:21,778
Yeah.
I'll be right there.

392
00:17:22,369 --> 00:17:23,054
Whew!

393
00:17:26,773 --> 00:17:27,592
Mike.

394
00:17:27,841 --> 00:17:29,055
Think I might have
pulled a ligament.

395
00:17:29,810 --> 00:17:30,709
Better walk this off.

396
00:17:34,293 --> 00:17:36,573
I can't believe
you're bailing on me.

397
00:17:36,608 --> 00:17:38,178
Football was your idea.

398
00:17:38,179 --> 00:17:39,949
Yep, and the knee
was my idea, too.

399
00:17:40,160 --> 00:17:40,957
I'm on a roll.

400
00:17:42,756 --> 00:17:46,609
Mike, they're not gonna play
if we don't go out there.

401
00:17:48,462 --> 00:17:50,223
Frankie, we can't do this
every day.

402
00:17:50,464 --> 00:17:52,141
It's not our job
to entertain them.

403
00:17:52,212 --> 00:17:54,127
Either they're gonna
get along or they won't.

404
00:17:54,128 --> 00:17:55,201
It's not up to us.

405
00:17:55,443 --> 00:17:57,048
You know what that
sounds like to me?

406
00:17:57,771 --> 00:18:01,032
The excuse of
a lazy, lazy parent.

407
00:18:01,442 --> 00:18:02,304
Well, you know what?

408
00:18:03,244 --> 00:18:05,649
I'm not gonna be lazy.

409
00:18:09,932 --> 00:18:11,699
Mom, you're not
even running.

410
00:18:12,010 --> 00:18:13,605
Well, you've gotta
throw the ball closer.

411
00:18:14,750 --> 00:18:16,474
<i>I wasn't sure</i>
<i>which was making me madder--</i>

412
00:18:16,695 --> 00:18:18,396
<i>that Mike was bailing</i>
<i>on the family</i>

413
00:18:18,431 --> 00:18:21,178
<i>by faking an injury</i>
<i>or that he thought of it first.</i>

414
00:18:25,545 --> 00:18:26,640
Mom, you've gotta run.

415
00:18:26,682 --> 00:18:29,018
<i>Did I?</i>
<i>Did I have to run?</i>

416
00:18:29,338 --> 00:18:31,017
<i>I was sick of</i>
<i>touch football.</i>

417
00:18:31,059 --> 00:18:33,639
<i>How the hell did the Kennedys</i>
<i>do it for so many years?</i>

418
00:18:33,640 --> 00:18:36,706
You know what? Boy, I really
should be making some calls

419
00:18:36,741 --> 00:18:38,544
about those wreaths,
but you kids keep playing.

420
00:18:39,079 --> 00:18:41,166
Forget it.
We're just gonna go watch TV.

421
00:18:41,201 --> 00:18:42,490
Ooh, I call the big TV!

422
00:18:42,883 --> 00:18:46,031
- You always get the big TV!
- No, no, stop!

423
00:18:46,144 --> 00:18:48,493
You can't go inside
just 'cause I'm going inside.

424
00:18:48,528 --> 00:18:49,262
Why not?

425
00:18:49,403 --> 00:18:51,636
Because you gotta stay out here
and bond, damn it.

426
00:18:52,330 --> 00:18:53,061
What? No way!

427
00:18:53,096 --> 00:18:55,690
I'm not gonna play with just
my stupid sister and dork brother.

428
00:18:56,478 --> 00:18:57,831
Hey, I'm not a dork!

429
00:18:57,970 --> 00:18:58,890
<i>Dork.</i>

430
00:18:59,126 --> 00:19:02,377
I was dragging my sorry butt
out here every night

431
00:19:02,412 --> 00:19:04,396
'cause I thought it would help
you kids love each other,

432
00:19:04,674 --> 00:19:07,976
but <i>no,</i> we're right back
to where we started!

433
00:19:08,011 --> 00:19:09,219
Well, you know
what I say?

434
00:19:09,558 --> 00:19:11,903
Love each other,
don't love each other.

435
00:19:11,938 --> 00:19:12,910
I'm done!

436
00:19:13,980 --> 00:19:14,461
Ooh!

437
00:19:15,149 --> 00:19:17,384
Aah! Ohh!

438
00:19:20,032 --> 00:19:21,119
Mom, are you okay?

439
00:19:21,120 --> 00:19:23,090
No, it hurts like hell!

440
00:19:25,425 --> 00:19:27,524
<i>So looks like my plan</i>
<i>to bring the kids closer</i>

441
00:19:27,605 --> 00:19:28,836
<i>hadn't worked the way</i>
<i>I wanted.</i>

442
00:19:29,057 --> 00:19:30,562
Come on, guys.
Stop laughing.

443
00:19:31,198 --> 00:19:32,739
That was awesome, Mom.

444
00:19:32,774 --> 00:19:36,068
Okay, I'm sorry, Mom,
but that was hilarious.

445
00:19:36,136 --> 00:19:38,444
And when you fell down,
you rolled through dog poo.

446
00:19:39,421 --> 00:19:41,571
<i>But I had given them a story</i>
<i>they were gonna share</i>

447
00:19:41,606 --> 00:19:43,540
<i>and enjoy</i>
<i>long after I was gone.</i>

448
00:19:44,178 --> 00:19:45,702
So then
she spiked the ball,

449
00:19:45,737 --> 00:19:47,790
and it bounced back
right off her face.

450
00:19:48,015 --> 00:19:49,179
Off her face?

451
00:19:49,762 --> 00:19:52,546
Yeah, and Axl does
this great impression.

452
00:19:52,798 --> 00:19:55,055
Do it, Axl.
It's really, really funny.

453
00:19:55,489 --> 00:19:57,305
Okay, okay. Well, okay.
So I'm Mom.

454
00:19:57,496 --> 00:19:58,608
So she's out there
and she's got the ball,

455
00:19:58,669 --> 00:20:00,137
"Ohh! Mom..."

456
00:20:00,172 --> 00:20:02,213
<i>Isn't that what bonds kids</i>
<i>after all--</i>

457
00:20:02,435 --> 00:20:04,289
<i>the eye rolling,</i>
<i>the laughing together</i>

458
00:20:04,360 --> 00:20:05,935
<i>over the stupid things</i>
<i>we do?</i>

459
00:20:06,367 --> 00:20:07,303
<i>I think so.</i>

460
00:20:08,006 --> 00:20:11,243
<i>Or maybe that's just the excuse</i>
<i>of a lazy, lazy parent.</i>

461
00:20:11,917 --> 00:20:14,830
<i>Either way,</i>
<i>I'm doing the best I can.</i>

462
00:20:15,876 --> 00:20:17,233
And I know you're
rolling your eyes,

463
00:20:17,305 --> 00:20:19,423
and yes, I'm a horrible mother,
but we have 30 wreaths

464
00:20:19,473 --> 00:20:21,290
to deliver by dinner,
so let's move it.

465
00:20:23,669 --> 00:20:25,307
Oh, wow!

466
00:20:25,719 --> 00:20:27,921
Look how helpful
your kids are.

467
00:20:27,956 --> 00:20:29,957
I've been watching you guys
play touch football.

468
00:20:30,379 --> 00:20:32,246
Wish my kids
got along like that.

469
00:20:32,637 --> 00:20:34,760
How do you do it,
Frankie?

470
00:20:35,051 --> 00:20:37,300
<i>I could have told her the truth</i>
<i>and spared her the pain</i>

471
00:20:37,420 --> 00:20:38,370
<i>I've gone through...</i>

472
00:20:38,480 --> 00:20:39,305
It's a lot of work,

473
00:20:39,944 --> 00:20:42,604
but Mike and I have just always
made family a priority.

474
00:20:44,204 --> 00:20:45,427
Oh, thanks.

